Z||000004||fierospeeder||08-16-2000||04:03 AM||||missing, such as a cylinder not really firing.<P>are all your plug wires pretty much new, if you got a timing light, you can check all four.<P>try not to run rich to long cause it can burn out your converter.||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||08-16-2000||01:58 PM||||I noticed that the 4th spark plug does have some carbon deposits on them, while the other three look perfect (a bit goldish). The plug wires are old, but they look ok?||||reg||1
Z||000006||whamel||08-16-2000||02:38 PM||||Things I have found this might be are other than what's already been mentioned:<BR>clogged air intake/filter/etc or manifold<BR>clogged exhaust/exit path<BR>(let's just say clogged air path)<BR>fuel leak (such as a line leak)<BR>bad plug or wires<BR>bad injectors or fuel pump or filter<BR>timing<BR>worn rings or liners<P>Does your car burn oil too?  Does it stumble or idle poorly?  I would check the plug wires again and make sure they are okay first, might save you some effort.  Just remember that a visual inspection may not be giving you the whole story - it may save you a lot of effort to check them thoroughly.  I had one go bad on my Lumina and you wouldn't know it from looking at it.  It looked perfect (no cracks or wear marks) - but when tested...it might as well have been cut in half.    Figures that it was one of the ones in the back..I hate rolling that engine out!<BR>     Remember, if you've got raw fuel escaping the engine, you may be eating your cat. convertor or worse very quickly.  Does your catalytic convertor get really, really hot?  Be careful - I can't remember how far the gas tank is from the convertor, but it's certainly too close for my tastes to let it go like that for very long.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||08-16-2000||03:35 PM||||Doesn't really look like it is burning oil. If it is, it's really slightly (actually, sometimes it looks like it is burning oil, and a lot of other times it doesn't)||||reg||1
